# =============================================================================
# api.http - one runnable HTTP request per endpoint
# =============================================================================
# WHAT THIS FILE IS:
# A plain text file where each block starting with "###" is one HTTP request.
# With the "REST Client" extension in VS Code (or the built-in HTTP client in
# IntelliJ IDEA), a "Send Request" link appears above each block - click it and
# the request is sent to your running application, with the response shown in a
# panel beside it. No Postman, no retyping curl commands.
#
# HOW TO USE IT:
#   1. Start the application:  mvn spring-boot:run   (from this folder)
#   2. Click "Send Request" above any block below.
#   3. Compare what comes back with the "Expected" note under each request.
#
# This file grows by one block every time a lesson adds an endpoint.
# =============================================================================

### Probe the server: is anything listening on port 8080?
# We have not written a single endpoint yet, so this SHOULD fail to find
# anything - and that failure is exactly the proof we want. A 404 means the
# server is alive, received our request, understood it, and correctly reported
# that nothing is mapped to "/". A refused connection would mean the server
# never started at all. Two very different outcomes, and telling them apart is
# the whole point of this request.
#
# Expected: HTTP/1.1 404
#           {"timestamp":"...","status":404,"error":"Not Found","path":"/"}
GET http://localhost:8080/
Accept: application/json


### The same probe, asking for HTML instead of JSON
# Identical request, one different header. Because we say we want HTML, Spring
# Boot's default error handler returns its "Whitelabel Error Page" - a small
# HTML page - instead of the JSON above. Same 404, same cause, different shape,
# chosen purely by what the caller said it could accept. This is a browser's
# view of the line above, and it is why hitting localhost:8080 in Chrome looks
# nothing like hitting it from this file.
#
# Expected: HTTP/1.1 404, an HTML page headed "Whitelabel Error Page"
GET http://localhost:8080/
Accept: text/html
